Patch: hotPath
Letter: G
Requires: none
Files: src/impl/formatter.js, src/impl/util.js, src/datetime.js, src/duration.js, src/impl/english.js, src/impl/diff.js, src/zones/systemZone.js
Summary: twelve fast paths: six along the route a numeric token takes, four constants that were being rebuilt per call, two Duration round trips on the arithmetic path

G: avoidable work at twelve points, in three families

Grouped, because it is one idea applied twelve times: each takes work
out of a step without changing what the step means. The grouping is by kind of
change, not by size, and the sizes are not close. Most of the twelve are worth
little alone — the smallest is worth nothing measurable on either engine — and
earn their place by adding up to most of what is left on a formatting path once
the Intl calls (A, B, D, E) are dealt with. The third family is not like that.
One of its two members is the largest single result in this whole set, and it is
here because it is the same kind of change rather than because it is the same
size.

Nothing in this patch is confined to formatting. The unit tables, the SystemZone
probe and both arithmetic round trips are under startOf, endOf, set, hasSame,
plus, minus, diff, toRelative and every Duration and Interval method, none of
which formats anything and none of which any other patch here touches.

The first six are the route every numeric token walks, in order:

  formatter.js  num()        a formatter whose numbering system is latn can pad
                             digits itself; PolyNumberFormatter's Intl branch is
                             only needed by the ones that cannot.
  formatter.js  parseFormat  splitting a format string into tokens is pure, and
                             the same few strings recur, so the split is cached
                             rather than redone per value.
  formatter.js  token loop   the loop rebuilt its own state per token; hoisting
                             what does not vary keeps the switch monomorphic.
  util.js       roundTo      every rounding mode is the identity on an integer
                             once digits is non-negative, and an integer is what
                             every numeric token arrives as.
  util.js       padStart     month, day, hour, minute and second all pad to two
                             digits, so a "00".."99" table covers the common
                             case without building either string.
  datetime.js   tsToObj      integer civil math in place of a wrapper Date and
                             seven getter calls. TimeClip is written out here
                             because the Date constructor used to run it: without
                             the truncation a fractional timestamp rendered as
                             "00:00:01.0.5", and without the range check a plus
                             that overflowed answered +287168-08-24 where luxon
                             says invalid. Both are reachable from ordinary
                             calls; see the note at the end.

Two of those six are engine-specific, and they are the weakest part of this
patch: the token loop pays off on V8 and gives a little back on JSC, and the
padStart table is the reverse. They are in because the other four carry the
change, and splitting by engine would mean filing six patches to land four.

H subsumes two of them by construction — parseFormat and the punctuation half of
the token loop — so a tree that already has H gets less from that half of this
patch than the list above suggests.

The next four are constants that were being built inside the function that
reads them, and are simply moved to module scope:

  datetime.js   normalizeUnit       a 24-key table, allocated to read one key
                                    and dropped, on every startOf, endOf, set,
                                    get, hasSame and ISO precision.
  duration.js   normalizeUnit       the same again, 18 keys, on every Duration
                                    built from an object — so on every plus,
                                    minus, diff, shiftTo, as and get.
  english.js    formatRelativeTime  an 8-key table and the nine arrays inside
                                    it, plus a three-element list, per call to
                                    toRelative or toRelativeCalendar.
  systemZone.js offset              one Date per offset lookup, replaced by one
                                    Date reused. JS is single threaded and
                                    getTimezoneOffset reads it on the next line,
                                    so the instance cannot be observed in
                                    between.

None of the four captures anything, so each is a move rather than a rewrite, and
no output changes: 82,016 comparisons across five zones, nine units, the unit
aliases and the throwing path are identical on both engines.

They are worth listing separately from the six because they reach somewhere the
rest of the ladder does not. A, B, D and E pay for themselves only when a zone is
named; H, C and the six above only when something is formatted or parsed. These
four land on arithmetic (startOf -3% node / -27% bun, plus -7% / -19%, hasSame
-8% / -22%), on relative time (toRelativeCalendar -9% / -22%), and on
SystemZone, which is the default zone and therefore the configuration none of
the tables in benchmarks/upstream.ts name.

The engine split runs the same way as it does for the six: V8 escape-analyzes
some of these allocations away and JSC does not, so node sees -2% to -11% where
bun sees -6% to -27%. A fifth candidate of the same shape, the table of closures
impl/diff.js builds per call in highOrderDiffs, is deliberately not here: it
measured as nothing on either engine, both apparently inlining it, and a change
that cannot be measured is not worth a reviewer reading it.

The last two are the same idea one level up: not a constant rebuilt per call but
a whole Duration built, converted and dropped to reach a number that plain
arithmetic already has.

  datetime.js  adjustTime  millisToAdd was
                           Duration.fromObject({ ...nine keys }).as("milliseconds"),
                           which is a nine-key normalizeObject, a Locale lookup,
                           a Duration allocation, a shiftTo walk over all nine
                           ordered units carrying two more objects, a
                           normalizeValues pass and a second Duration out of
                           shiftTo's clone. Under --cpu-prof that one expression
                           is about 53% of self time in plus, against 7% for
                           adjustTime's own body.
  diff.js      dayDiff     ended in Math.floor(Duration.fromMillis(ms).as("days")),
                           where shiftTo's leftover loop makes as("days") the
                           single division this now does.

Neither is an unconditional replacement, because as() is not the plain sum it
looks like. Two guards, both in the patch as comments:

Whole values. In adjustTime the five calendar fields contribute only their
fractional parts, so when all nine fields are integers those terms are exactly
zero and shiftTo's accumulator reduces to the same left-to-right sum. Its
trunc-and-remainder split then round-trips exactly, which it does not do in
general. Anything fractional still takes the old path.

Range. When the sum leaves the finite range shiftTo's remainder goes NaN, and the
milliseconds getter's `|| 0` turns that into zero — so today
plus({ seconds: 1e308 }) adds nothing rather than going invalid. That looks like
a bug, but correcting it is a separate argument from this one, so the guard
preserves it. Finding it took 1,200 parity failures.

These two are why this patch is worth more than its position as the ladder's
last rung suggests, and they land where the ladder is otherwise silent: adjustTime
is under every plus and minus, and therefore under endOf, hasSame, diff,
toRelative and Interval#splitBy as well. Measured on the all-8 build, fastest of
five, node then bun: plus -80% / -64%, minus -79% / -62%, endOf -69% / -50%,
Interval#splitBy -75% / -60%, hasSame -59% / -40%, diff -55% / -28%, toRelative
-40% / -23%, with startOf and fromISO flat as controls. The dayDiff half of that
is small and narrow — a further -13% on diff and -6% on splitBy, nothing anywhere
else. Replacing the inst.c spread in adjustTime with a literal is in the same
hunk and measured at 1-2%, which is inside the run-to-run noise; it is here
because it is free and fixes the object's shape, not because it can be shown to
pay.

Verification is in benchmarks/test/hot-path-patch.test.ts, plus 85,806
patched-against-patched comparisons over five zones, 32 duration shapes covering
fractional, negative, past-2^53 and non-finite values, and DST edges. Luxon's own
suite was also run against a tree with this applied and produced an identical
pass/fail set.

One correction, not an optimization, and not to anything above: writing that
sweep turned up two bugs in the tsToObj rewrite in the first list, both from the
same cause. Replacing new Date(ts) with civil math also dropped the TimeClip the
Date constructor was running on the way in, which used to truncate the timestamp
toward zero and return NaN outside +/-MAX_DATE. Without the truncation,
DateTime.fromSeconds(1.0005).toISO() rendered "1970-01-01T00:00:01.0.5Z", a
malformed string rather than a merely different one, and fromMillis(-0.5) moved a
whole day, because Date rounds toward zero and Math.floor does not. Without the
range check, fromMillis(0).plus({ milliseconds: 9e15 }).toISO() answered
"+287168-08-24T16:00:00.000Z" where luxon says null — calendar-unit overflow is
caught by the Date.UTC inside objToLocalTS, but millisToAdd is added to the
timestamp after that, so nothing was checking it.

Neither was reachable from any benchmark, which is why both survived: every
timestamp a benchmark builds is a whole number well inside the range. Both are
reachable from ordinary calls. TimeClip is now written out at the top of tsToObj,
and both cases are in the test sweep.

